<p>Over the years, you’ve probably heard at least a few people casually describe themselves as Epicureans. As used today, the label can refer to as little as enjoying the occasional expensive restaurant meal. The bar was a bit higher in the second century, as explained by ancient-history YouTuber Garrett Ryan in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XZ85zOf0EQU">the new video above from his channel <em>Told in Stone</em></a>. But there is, at least, one major aspect of the original doctrine laid out by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epicurus">Epicurus</a> (341–270 BC) with which most any of his self-styled followers in our time would agree: that pleasure is the greatest good. Whether they would follow that premise all the way to the conclusions Epicurus did is another matter.</p>
<p>For that ancient Greek philosopher, Ryan explains, “happiness was the sole legitimate goal of human existence. The essence of happiness is freedom from anxiety. Since the greatest source of mental disturbance is fear about death and the afterlife, it is necessary to understand the true nature of the universe.”</p>
